{"id":"wp-2106780","title":"Mount Amana","summary":"Mount Amana (אֲמָנָה, a-mā'na, a-mä'na, uh-may'nuh) is an ancient name for the southern Anti-Lebanon Mountains.","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"","level":1,"text":"Mount Amana (אֲמָנָה, a-mā'na, a-mä'na, uh-may'nuh) is an ancient name for the southern Anti-Lebanon Mountains.","links":[],"citationIds":["cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrr1915113-1","cite_note-FOOTNOTEDouglasTenneySilva201155-2"],"category":"overview","truncated":false,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918"},{"title":"Geography","anchor":"Geography","level":2,"text":"Mount Amana is at the southern end of the Anti-Lebanon Mountains, near the source of the river Abana. Paul Haupt identifies this mountain as Jabal az-Zabadany, northwest of Damascus.\n\nMount Amana is often confused with Mount Amanus, also known as Mount Hor, at the north end of the Syrian plain.","links":[],"citationIds":["cite_note-FOOTNOTEEaston1897-3","cite_note-FOOTNOTERand2015-4","cite_note-FOOTNOTEFreedmanMyers200049-5","cite_note-FOOTNOTEWaltonMatthewsChavalas2012391-6","cite_note-FOOTNOTEWellsCalmet1817269Abana-7","cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrr1915113-1","cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaupt19028-8","cite_note-FOOTNOTEGill1763-9","cite_note-10"],"category":"geography","truncated":false,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#Geography"},{"title":"Notable mentions","anchor":"Notable_mentions","level":2,"text":"Mount Amana is mentioned in Song of Songs (4:8) along with Lebanon, Senir, and Mount Hermon. Senir, Mount Hermon, and Amana are all prominent mountains on the northern end of Israel in the Anti-Lebanon Mountains. In this era, Lebanon referred to both the Lebanese Mountains and the Anti-Lebanese mountains without referring to any particular peak. A targum on this verse reads \"They that dwell on the river Amana shall offer thee a gift.\"\n\nThe \"mountains of Sanir and Amana\" are also mentioned in the Book of Jubilees as lying within the inheritance of Shem (8:21), or more specifically, Arpachshad (9:4).\n\nWinckler was the first scholar to suggest that the Mount Ammananu referred to in the inscriptions of Tiglath-pileser III should be understood as identical with Amanah, a claim which has been confirmed by more recent scholarship.\n\nTacitus records that a triumphal arch was erected on Mount Amana (possibly Mount Amanus) in honor of Germanicus after his death.","links":[],"citationIds":["cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrr1915113-1","cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlochBloch199513-11","cite_note-FOOTNOTECogan1984255-12","cite_note-FOOTNOTEIkeda1978-13","cite_note-FOOTNOTEWellsCalmet1817269Abana-7","cite_note-FOOTNOTECogan1984256-14","cite_note-FOOTNOTEWinckler1892131n._1-15","cite_note-FOOTNOTECogan1984-16","cite_note-FOOTNOTEKraeling200846-17","cite_note-FOOTNOTETacitus1876-18"],"category":"overview","truncated":false,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#Notable_mentions"},{"title":"Amana River","anchor":"Amana_River","level":2,"text":"In the Masoretic Text of the Hebrew Bible, the name \"Amana\" is given in the margin to 2 Kings 5:12 as an alternate reading of Abana, and contemporary scholars prefer the reading Amana, following the targum. This river flows through Damascus and is currently known as the Barada.","links":[{"label":"2 Kings 5:12","url":"https://bible.oremus.org/?passage=2%20Kings%205:12&version=nrsv"}],"citationIds":["cite_note-FOOTNOTEWaltonMatthewsChavalas2012391-6","cite_note-FOOTNOTEEaston1897-3","cite_note-FOOTNOTECogan1984-16","cite_note-FOOTNOTEGill1763-9","cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaupt19028-8","cite_note-FOOTNOTEKraeling200846-17","cite_note-FOOTNOTECogan1984255-12","cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaparchi1852-19","cite_note-FOOTNOTEPorter1855-20"],"category":"overview","truncated":false,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#Amana_River"},{"title":"Meanings","anchor":"Meanings","level":2,"text":"The name Amana means \"constant\", \"firm\", \"faith\", \"truth\", \"credulity\", or \"a nurse\". It was translated in the Septuagint as πιστεως, meaning \"trust\", \"fidelity\", or \"faithfulness\"","links":[],"citationIds":["cite_note-FOOTNOTEDouglasTenneySilva201155-2","cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrr1915113-1","cite_note-FOOTNOTEWellsCalmet1817276Amana-21","cite_note-22"],"category":"overview","truncated":false,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#Meanings"}],"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Amana","revision":1350129918,"revisionUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918","historyUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mount_Amana&action=history","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","attribution":"Wikipedia contributors","retrievedAt":"2026-10-20T13:16:04+00:00","source":{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=parse&prop=text%7Csections%7Cexternallinks%7Crevid%7Cproperties&page=Mount+Amana&redirects=1&format=json&maxlag=5","sha256":"3aea4e3664fb1a62979b2853e5b7509b63fd9ab6e56441e01f9aa95d0c416159"},"references":[{"id":"cite_note-10","text":"^ eg. Orr 1915, p. 113; Robinson 1835, p. 51; Schwarz 1850, p. 19","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#cite_note-10"},{"id":"cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrr1915113-1","text":"^ a b c d e f Orr 1915, p. 113.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrr1915113-1"},{"id":"cite_note-FOOTNOTEDouglasTenneySilva201155-2","text":"^ a b Douglas, Tenney & Silva 2011, p. 55.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDouglasTenneySilva201155-2"},{"id":"cite_note-FOOTNOTEEaston1897-3","text":"^ a b c Easton 1897.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEaston1897-3"},{"id":"cite_note-FOOTNOTERand2015-4","text":"^ a b Rand 2015.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1350129918#cite_note-FOOTNOTERand2015-4"},{"id":"cite_note-FOOTNOTEFreedmanMyers200049-5","text":"^ Freedman & Myers 